<!--
  modules/javasci/pom.xml — Stage 2-f Task 2 module POM (Wave B)
  (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-stage2f-remaining-modules.md).

  STATUS: ADDITIVE and RUN BY HAND (mvn -pl modules/javasci -am package
  from the reactor root, or mvn -f modules/javasci/pom.xml package). Ant
  (modules/javasci/build.xml + build.incl.xml) remains the real build;
  nothing here is wired into CMake or any release build yet.

  WHY THIS MODULE. Part of Wave B, and the smallest of the three (5 source
  files). Depends on nothing but types (Stage 2-f Task 1, Wave A) — but its
  BUILD.XML is the one genuine surprise in this wave; read on before
  assuming this is a copy of modules/localization/pom.xml with a different
  <artifactId>.

  STEP 1 FINDINGS (./maven-module-deps.sh javasci, 5 files):
    [1] imports: org.scilab.modules.types.{GetScilabVariable, ScilabType,
        ScilabTypeEnum, ScilabIntegerTypeEnum, ScilabDouble, ScilabList,
        ScilabMList, ScilabTList, ScilabString, ScilabBoolean,
        ScilabBooleanSparse, ScilabInteger, ScilabPolynomial, ScilabSparse,
        ScilabVariables, ScilabVariablesHandler} — every one confirmed by
        reading all 5 files directly (Call_Scilab.java, Scilab.java,
        ScilabVariablesJavasci.java); types migrated Stage 2-f Task 1
        (Wave A). No other Scilab module referenced anywhere, including no
        org.scilab.modules.jvm import — see "THE BESPOKE MANIFEST" below for
        why that absence matters.
    [2] reflection targets: (none).
    [3] fully-qualified uses with no import: none beyond [1] (every
        org.scilab.modules.types.* name is a real, direct import).
  No thirdparty jar of any kind on javasci's own compile classpath — the
  smallest dependency footprint of any module migrated so far.

  THE BESPOKE MANIFEST — the actual reason this module gets its own STEP 1
  writeup instead of a two-line diff against modules/localization/pom.xml.
  modules/javasci/build.xml does NOT import build.incl.xml's shared
  `compile`/`jar` targets for this module the way every other build.xml
  does — it DEFINES ITS OWN `compile` and `jar` targets under the same
  names, which (Ant <import> semantics: a target defined in the importING
  file always wins over one of the same name from an imported file) silently
  REPLACE the shared ones instead of extending them:

      <target name="jar" description="Build the jar file" depends="compile">
          <manifestclasspath property="manifest.classpath"
                              jarfile="${build.jar.dir}/${library.name}">
              <classpath location="${library.jvm.dir}/${library.jvm.name}"/>
          </manifestclasspath>
          <jar destfile="${build.jar.dir}/${library.name}" basedir="${classes.dir}/v2/">
              <manifest>
                  <attribute name="Class-Path" value="${manifest.classpath}"/>
              </manifest>
          </jar>
      </target>

  Confirmed by extracting the ACTUAL Ant-built jar's manifest (unzip -p
  modules/javasci/jar/org.scilab.modules.javasci.jar META-INF/MANIFEST.MF):

      Manifest-Version: 1.0
      Ant-Version: Apache Ant 1.10.17
      Created-By: 25.0.3+9-LTS-195 (Oracle Corporation)
      Class-Path: ../../jvm/jar/org.scilab.modules.jvm.jar

  Contrast modules/renderer/pom.xml's or modules/console/pom.xml's Ant
  manifest: Built-By, a "Name: org/scilab/modules/<m>/" section header, and
  Specification-Title/Version/Vendor + Implementation-Title/Version/Vendor
  are ALL ABSENT here — not stripped by parity/fingerprint.py's
  _MANIFEST_VOLATILE (that only removes specific lines that exist), simply
  never written, because javasci's own `jar` target never runs
  build.incl.xml's shared one (build.incl.xml:148-163) that would have
  produced them. This is a THIRD, distinct manifest shape in this reactor —
  NOT the same case modules/scirenderer/pom.xml documents as "2 of 24
  modules declare manifest.class-path" (that count is scirenderer + gui,
  both of which DO run the shared jar target and merely set its
  `manifest.class-path` property to a non-default value). javasci sets a
  DIFFERENTLY-NAMED property (`manifest.classpath`, no hyphen, computed by
  the <manifestclasspath> task from the two jars' actual relative
  filesystem locations, modules/javasci/jar/ -> modules/jvm/jar/) inside a
  wholly bespoke jar target that skips the shared one's Built-By/Name/
  Specification/Implementation logic entirely. It is not counted among
  those two, and would not be counted among a hypothetical three either —
  it is a different mechanism, not a third instance of the same one.

  CONSEQUENCES FOR THIS POM, both deliberate reproductions of what Ant
  actually does, not omissions:

  1) <dependencies> below carries only `types` — NOT `jvm`, even though the
     manifest names jvm's jar. The Class-Path entry is a DEPLOYMENT-time
     classpath hint (where to find jvm's classes alongside javasci's own at
     runtime); it has nothing to do with COMPILE-time symbol resolution, and
     javasci's source never imports anything from org.scilab.modules.jvm
     (confirmed above). Adding jvm as a Maven <dependency> here would put a
     jar on the compile classpath that Ant's own compile target
     (modules/javasci/build.xml's own bespoke `compile` target, which
     compiles against the SAME shared compile.classpath as every other
     module regardless of the jar target being bespoke) already provides
     but that this module's source never asks for — not wrong, but not what
     Ant's actual dependency shape is either, and every other module in
     this migration adds a reactor dependency ONLY on confirmed source
     evidence.

  2) THE MANIFEST FRAGMENT (src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF) is NOT
     the usual "Name: ... / Specification-* / Implementation-* / Class-Path"
     block. It is exactly one line — a MAIN-section attribute, not a named
     section — extracted byte-for-byte (never retyped) from the Ant jar
     above:

         Class-Path: ../../jvm/jar/org.scilab.modules.jvm.jar

     This is the SAME "reproduce Ant's OUTPUT bytes literally, do not
     compute a fresh value for Maven's own target/ directory layout"
     principle already established for modules/console/pom.xml's and
     modules/renderer/pom.xml's literal "${manifest.class-path}" — the
     value is copied as a fixed string regardless of where Maven's own jar
     ends up on disk, because Maven's output is not wired into anything
     that reads this Class-Path entry today (Global Constraints:
     ADDITIVE). No leading blank line precedes it (contrast every other
     migrated module's fragment): a blank line signals "a NEW section
     starts here" to the manifest parser, and this fragment has no section
     to start — it merges directly into maven-archiver's own auto-generated
     main-section attributes (Manifest-Version, plus whatever Built-By the
     parent POM's manifestEntries contributes).

  3) NO Implementation-Version LINE — a deliberate DEVIATION from this
     stage's general instruction that it "must be PRESENT... positioned
     exactly between Implementation-Title and Implementation-Vendor",
     because for THIS module those two anchor attributes do not exist on
     either side of the comparison: Ant's real jar has neither, so there is
     no "between" to place anything in. Adding a fabricated Name:/
     Specification-*/Implementation-* block to manufacture a place for it
     would be a REAL, non-volatile divergence from Ant's actual manifest
     content (extra lines with no counterpart on the Ant side,
     none of them matched by _MANIFEST_VOLATILE) and would fail parity —
     exactly the "the POM is wrong" case Global Constraints warns about,
     not a harness gap to route around. This is also not a regression
     introduced by this migration: getImplementationVersion() already
     returns null for org/scilab/modules/javasci/ under the CURRENT,
     unmodified Ant build, because Ant's own bespoke jar target for this
     module has never populated it. Reproducing that absence is the
     correct application of "reproduce, don't improve" here, not an
     oversight — flagged loudly in the Task 2 report per this stage's
     instructions, the same way an unexpected <finalName> override would
     be.

  4) Built-By: still safe to leave to the parent's inherited
     manifestEntries (no override here) even though Ant's real javasci
     manifest has no Built-By line at all — unlike Implementation-Version,
     Built-By IS matched by parity/fingerprint.py's _MANIFEST_VOLATILE by
     attribute NAME alone (`^(Ant-Version|Created-By|Built-By|...)`),
     regardless of value or even presence on the other side, so Maven
     carrying one where Ant has none is invisible to the parity gate.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.scilab</groupId>
    <artifactId>scilab-modules-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2027.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>javasci</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <!-- build.xml:20 <property name="library.title" value="Scilab Java API"/> -->
  <name>Scilab Java API</name>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- org.scilab.modules.types.* — every one of javasci's 5 source files
         resolves to this single reactor dependency; see this file's header
         comment for why jvm is deliberately NOT listed here despite
         appearing in the Ant manifest's Class-Path entry. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.scilab</groupId>
      <artifactId>types</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <!-- build.incl.xml:22 <property name="src.dir" location="src/java/"/>,
         reproduced by modules/javasci/build.xml's OWN bespoke `compile`
         target too (srcdir="src/java/", destdir="${classes.dir}/v2" —
         "v2" is only Ant's local BUILD-STAGING subdirectory name,
         invisible in the packaged jar: `<jar basedir="${classes.dir}/v2/">`
         packs that directory's CONTENTS at the jar root, i.e.
         org/scilab/modules/javasci/*.class, byte-for-byte the same layout
         Maven's own target/classes/ produces). Same source tree either
         way; nothing module-specific to add here. -->
    <sourceDirectory>src/java</sourceDirectory>

    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <!-- addMavenDescriptor=false is inherited from the parent POM.
                 manifestFile mechanism as every other migrated module — but
                 THIS FRAGMENT'S SHAPE IS UNIQUE. See this file's header
                 comment ("THE BESPOKE MANIFEST") for the full reasoning:
                 one main-section Class-Path attribute, extracted verbatim
                 from the Ant-built jar's actual manifest (unzip -p
                 modules/javasci/jar/org.scilab.modules.javasci.jar
                 META-INF/MANIFEST.MF), no Name:/Specification-*/
                 Implementation-* block, and deliberately NO
                 Implementation-Version line. -->
            <manifestFile>${project.basedir}/src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF</manifestFile>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
